2207395	Welcome to the Homeworld: Cataclysm tutorial.
2207396	This tutorial is divided into 12 lessons. Each lesson has objectives listed in the bottom left corner. As in the regular singleplayer game, clicking on these will provide you with detailed information about an objective.
2207397	If at any time you need more information on how to complete a lesson objective, clicking the objective listing will give you instructions.
2207398	If you are familiar with Homeworld and know how to complete an objective without explanation, you can do so. You can even skip the objective altogether, though a refresher never hurts.
2207399	You can proceed to the next lesson at any time by clicking the button labeled NEXT LESSON. You can restart a lesson or go back to a previous lesson by clicking the button labeled RESTART LESSON.
2207400	Pressing the [BACKSPACE] key will skip from one speech event to the next.
2207401	For this tutorial, it is best if your key configuration is set to the game's default.
2207402	If you have altered your key bindings in the Options screen prior to beginning the tutorial, you can reset them by pressing [ESC]. This will bring up the Game menu. From there you can access the key configuration settings by clicking the OPTIONS button.
2207403	Good. The objective for Lesson One is now complete. Click the NEXT LESSON button to move on to Lesson Two.
2207404	This lesson deals with controlling the camera in Cataclysm. Click an objective to continue with the lesson.
2207405	To move the camera view around, click and hold the RIGHT MOUSE BUTTON. Then, by moving the mouse left or right you can rotate the camera horizontally relative to your ships.
2207406	By clicking and holding the RIGHT MOUSE BUTTON and then moving the mouse up or down you can move the camera into an overhead or underside view of your ships.
2207407	The camera can be zoomed in and out relative to your ship. Hold BOTH MOUSE BUTTONS and move the mouse up and down to move the camera in and out. If you have a MOUSE WHEEL, rolling it will do the same thing.
2207408	This lesson deals with selecting ships. When you issue a command, it is issued to all of the ships currently selected. Click an objective to continue with the lesson.
2207409	Left clicking on a ship by itself will select that ship. If the ship is in formation with other ships, left clicking it will select all the ships in the group. Clicking the ship a second time will select that individual ship.
2207410	Note the green bar above your selected ships. This bar indicates the general condition of your ships. Green means a ship is in good working order. Yellow indicates damage and that repairs are required. Red indicates that the ship is badly damaged and steps must be taken right away to avoid the destruction of the ship.
2207411	Zoom the camera out to view the rest of your fleet. Then click and hold the left mouse button and then drag the pointer across one or more ships to select them. Try selecting a mixed group of ships.
2207412	Notice the selected ship list in the top right corner...
2207413	This list not only tells you what you currently have selected, but you can use it to select a particular ship type. Click on the first ship type in the list.
2207414	If you have selected one or more ships, you can add more ships to that selection by holding down the [Shift] key and selecting the additional ships you want added.
2207415	Band-select some ships to continue. Click and hold the left mouse button and then drag the pointer across one or more ships to select them.
2207416	This lesson covers focusing the camera in Cataclysm. Click an objective to continue with the lesson.
2207417	To focus the camera on any ships you have selected, press the [F] key.
2207418	The first time you focus, the view will center on the ship. A second time will move the view in close to the selected ships.
2207419	Alternately, you can hold the [Alt] key and left click on an object to focus in close on it. This [ALT] focus allows you to focus the camera on most anything in the game, including your ships, enemy ships, even asteroids and debris.
2207420	Remember that once you have focused the camera you can use the RIGHT MOUSE BUTTON to rotate the camera around and either the MOUSE WHEEL or BOTH MOUSE BUTTONS to zoom in and out.
2207421	To focus in on a group of items, hold down the [Alt] key, click and hold the left mouse button, and then drag the pointer across the items on which you want to focus the camera.
2207422	Right click the mouse pointer anywhere on the screen to bring up the Right Click Menu. This menu is used to issue commands to selected ships, switch views, and more. Almost any action can be controlled through the Right Click Menu.
2207423	This lesson covers the Sensors Manager, which is a broad tactical overview of the mission area, and gives an introduction to the Right Click Menu.
2207424	The Sensors Manager can be accessed through the Right Click Menu by selecting the heading VIEW and then the sub heading SENSORS. Alternately, you can activate the Sensors Manager by pressing the space bar.
2207425	This is the Sensors Manager. All of the commands you can issue from the main view, such as selecting, attacking, or moving, can also be issued here within the Sensors Manager. These commands will be discussed later.
2207426	This is your command ship.
2207427	All ships appearing in the Sensors Manager are color-coded. Your ships appear in green.
2207428	Hostile ships are red.
2207429	Friendly ships, such as Allied ships, ships manned by other Kiith, or Taiidan Republican ships appear as blue.
2207430	Asteroids appear as brown dots.
2207431	This is a 'ping'. 'Pings' only show up in Sensors Manager, and are used to point out important items like significant ships or areas of space.
2207432	Let's have a look at the Sensors Manager sub-functions. The blue area denotes the range for your active sensors.
2207433	Active sensors only give useful readings relatively close to the ships of the fleet. Enemy ships can only be spotted within active sensor range. Friendly ships can be seen beyond this range as they are sending you their location coordinates.
2207434	The light colored framework is a display of the approximate range for passive sensors. As you move ships around, this framework will change accordingly to show what areas have been explored.
2207435	The passive sensors record the position of resources within their scan range, but passive sensors use such a weak signal that you cannot see if an explored area has changed until a ship is sent through the area again.
2207436	These are weak sensor returns from beyond passive sensor range. You can use these returns as a landmark of sorts to help visualize the position of your fleet in an area of space. These weak returns are often good indications of where to look for resources.
2207437	Activate the Sensors Manager to continue. The Sensors Manger can be accessed through the Right Click Menu under the heading VIEW and then the sub-heading of SENSORS. Alternately, you can activate the Sensors Manager by pressing the [Space Bar].
2207438	You can exit Sensors Manager by using the Right Click Menu and selecting the heading VIEW, then the sub-heading Main Region. You can also exit Sensors Manager by pressing the [Space Bar], or by selecting one or more ships and focusing on them.
2207439	This lesson deals with moving ships and setting up patrol paths using waypoints. Click an objective to continue with the lesson.
2207440	If you have ships selected, you can activate the Right Click Menu and select MOVE from the menu.
2207441	This is the Movement Manager. It will be locked open for the time being in order to demonstrate its usage.
2207442	Once you have the pointer where you would like your ships to go and you are ready to have them move out, click the LEFT MOUSE BUTTON.
2207443	You can still click and hold the RIGHT MOUSE BUTTON to change your view and click and hold BOTH MOUSE BUTTONS - or the MOUSE WHEEL if you have it - to zoom in and out, even while the Movement Manager is active. This is often handy when trying to place the destination point in a particular area.
2207444	Waypoints are a series of points in space which make up a path for a ship to move along. As an example, you can use waypoints to set up patrol paths, define a search pattern, or to move your ships around and away from large enemy forces.
2207445	Go into the Right Click Menu and select START WAYPOINTS.
2207446	This is the Waypoint Manager. It works exactly the same as the Movement Manager, only every time you LEFT CLICK a specific point, the Manager will mark the point and remain open so you can add new points to your waypoint path.
2207447	There are three types of waypoint paths. Linear waypoint mode will direct the ships to follow the waypoints and wait at the end.
2207448	End To End waypoint mode will instruct the ships to follow the waypoint path to the end, turn around, and follow the waypoints back again. The ships will continue to follow the path like this until they are instructed otherwise.
2207449	Circular waypoint mode will instruct the ships to follow the waypoints, then, when they reach the final waypoint, to head back to the first point and start following the path again, making a closed patrol loop. The ships will continue to follow this path until they are given other orders.
2207450	These modes can be set by opening the Right Click Menu, selecting the heading START WAYPOINTS, and then selecting the waypoint mode you want from the sub-headings.
2207451	Once you have created the waypoint pattern you want, pressing the [W] key will close the waypoint manager and set the selected ships along that path.
2207452	This lesson teaches how to issue attack commands. Click an objective to continue with the lesson.
2207453	When you have combat vessels selected and you move your mouse over enemy ships, you will see the pointer change. This new pointer icon means the ship is an enemy target. By left clicking on it, you will be ordering your selected ships to attack.
2207454	You can also hold the [Ctrl] key, click and hold the LEFT MOUSE BUTTON, and drag a bandbox around two or more enemy ships to attack multiple targets. This can be done in the main game view as well as within the Sensors Manager.
2207455	This lesson covers how to build ships. Ship construction is done in the Build Manager.
2207456	You can access the Build Manager either through the VIEW options in the Right Click Menu, or by pressing [B].
2207457	Activate the Build Manager now.
2207458	Start by selecting Worker from the build menu. Clicking on any ship from the list will highlight it as the ship you’d like to build.
2207459	By continuing to click the ship you have selected, you will increase the number of that type of ship you’d like to build.
2207460	You can also set the number of ships you wish to build by clicking the increase arrow.
2207461	The decrease arrow will obviously set a lower number if you want to change the number of ships to build. Or you can repeatedly RIGHT CLICK on the ship you have selected.
2207462	When you are ready to build, you can start construction by pressing the BUILD BUTTON.
2207463	This gauge indicates your Support Unit usage. The maximum size of a fleet is measured by Support Units. Different ships have different Support Unit requirements. An Acolyte, for instance, requires fewer Support Units than a larger ship, such as a Dreadnought.
2207464	As you increase the number of ships to build, the gauge reflects the number of support units you have remaining. You can increase your total support units by researching and building Support Modules.
2207465	You can pause or cancel building at any time by selecting ships being built and pressing the PAUSE or CANCEL buttons. You can resume paused builds by pressing the PAUSE button again.
2207466	Highlighting a ship from the list and clicking the INFO button turns on or off useful statistics and information about that ship.
2207467	All of your command critical functions are tied into one another. You can move from the Build Manager to the Research and Systems Managers, and back again, through these buttons here.
2207468	This lesson covers the three important functions of a Worker - harvesting resources, repairing ships, and salvaging. These three special abilities are good examples of context-sensitive actions. By learning how to use the Workers' special abilities, you will learn how to use those of the other ships in your fleet.
2207469	Note that the Workers you have here in the tutorial are already upgraded with Repair and Salvaging technologies. Outside of this tutorial, in order to use these abilities, the appropriate technology must be researched, and Workers must then be properly upgraded. Researching and upgrading will be covered later. Click an objective to continue with the lesson.
2207470	This is an example of a special action. When the mouse cursor changes from an arrow to an icon, this indicates a special action is available. The action can then be triggered by clicking the RIGHT MOUSE BUTTON.
2207471	For instance, when the mouse pointer is moved over an asteroid while Workers are selected, the cursor will turn into a shovel, indicating that clicking the RIGHT MOUSE BUTTON will issue a Harvest command.
2207472	If there is no special action available, right clicking will bring up the Right Click Menu.
2207473	Another way to perform special actions is to hold down the [Z] key, then left click and drag the mouse pointer across the ships or items that you wish to affect.
2207474	By harvesting, you acquire Resource Units, or RU's, which are used to build ships and modules. To direct Workers to harvest, select your Workers, then move the mouse pointer over an asteroid or other resource and click the RIGHT MOUSE BUTTON.
2207475	Alternately, you can select Harvest from the Right Click Menu or press the [H] key. The Workers will then automatically harvest any resources that your sensors have revealed.
2207476	When Workers are upgraded with Repair technology, you can select them and then right click on a damaged ship to repair it. In addition to using Workers, ships can be repaired automatically when they are docked.
2207477	Select the Workers you want to salvage with. Sometimes a larger salvage item will require several Workers - salvaging the Ion Cannon Frigate requires 2.
2207478	In order to salvage a ship, the ship must be brought down below 50% health. You can tell when a ship is sufficiently damaged when flames begin to vent from its hull. The Ion Cannon Frigate is adequately damaged, and is ready to be salvaged.
2207479	Right click on the ship or item you want to salvage, and the Worker will begin salvaging.
2207480	This lesson covers advanced topics in attacking, such as how to put a selection of ships into formation, and how to set tactics. Click an objective to continue with the lesson.
2207481	To set ships into a formation, select the ships you wish to set, then select Formations from the Right Click Menu, and select the desired formation. In this case, set the formation to Delta.
2207482	A ship's tactics determine how it will react in a combat situation.
2207483	When set to Aggressive tactics, a ship will actively pursue and attack any enemy it encounters.
2207484	When set to Neutral tactics, a ship will passively defend itself, but will not perform any others actions without orders.
2207485	When set to Evasive tactics, a ship will flee any enemy it encounters.
2207486	To set a ship's tactics, select one or more ships, select TACTICS from the Right Click Menu, and then select the desired Tactics setting. In this case, set the tactics to AGGRESSIVE.
2207487	You can issue the Guard command from anywhere, including the Sensors Manager.
2207488	Select the escort ships you intend to guard with, then hold the [G] key and bandbox select the ships you want the escort ships to guard. The escort ships will then move to protect the ship they’re guarding.
2207489	You can assign ships to a hotkey group for easy selection later. To do this, select the ships you want to group. Then, press [Ctrl] and a number from one to nine. This will be the number of the group. You can quickly select all the ships in this group at any time by pressing whatever number you assigned to the group.
2207490	This lesson covers how to research upgrades for your fleet. This is done through the Research Manager.
2207491	You can activate the Research Manager from the Right Click Menu by selecting the heading VIEW, and the sub-heading RESEARCH MANAGER. Alternately, you can access the Research Manager by pressing the [R] key.
2207492	Activate the Research Manager now.
2207493	This is the Research Manager. You can move from the Research Manager to the other command function managers and back again through these buttons.
2207494	The Research Manager works a lot like the Build Manager. When you want to research a technology, click on it in the list on the left.
2207495	You can then DOUBLE CLICK the technology or click the RESEARCH button below to begin research.
2207496	As in the Build Manager, clicking the INFO button will give more information on selected technologies.
2207497	Link Technology research has been completed. Any new Acolytes you build will have this technology. As well, you can now upgrade previously built Acolytes.
2207498	Click the CLOSE button to exit Research Manager.
2207499	Now selecting an Acolyte and focusing in close on it...
2207500	Notice the blinking yellow dot next to the ship's condition bar.
2207501	This is an indicator that a ship has upgrades available.
2207502	Larger ships, like the Command Ship, can have their crews execute upgrades in the field. In the case of small ships, they need to dock for upgrades.
2207503	This is done via the Upgrade command in the Right Click Menu.
2207504	This command, like the yellow indicator dot, will only appear when there are technologies available for upgrades.
2207505	Select your upgraded Acolytes, and from the Right Click Menu, select the heading SPECIAL ACTIONS and then the sub-heading LINK.
2207506	This lesson covers various other features of Homeworld: Cataclysm. Click an objective to continue with the lesson.
2207507	The Tactical Overlay shows ship types, paths, and other useful information. To turn the Tactical Overlay on or off, choose VIEW and the sub-heading TACTICAL OVERLAY from the Right Click Menu.
2207508	The Ship Info Overlay is used to find out useful information about a ship, such as health, current upgrades, and weapon information.
2207509	To turn on the Info Overlay, use the Right Click Menu, and, under the heading VIEW, choose the sub-heading SHIP INFO OVERLAY. Then, any time you have a single ship selected, its info overlay will appear. You can turn it off again the same way.
2207510	To turn the pilot's-eye view on or off, use the Right Click Menu, and, from under the heading VIEW, choose the sub-heading PILOT VIEW.
2207511	To retire a ship, choose RETIRE from the Right Click Menu. Retiring ships frees up Support Units, and also returns a small amount of Resource Units to your RU total.
2207512	If you are worried about your ship being captured, or you need the Support Units, you can order a ship to scuttle itself.
2207513	To order a self-destruct, or scuttle, of a ship, select the ship and hit the [Shift] plus the [S] keys. The ship will then ask for confirmation. If you then hit the [Shift] plus the [S] keys again quickly, it will self destruct. Confirmation is required to prevent the accidental destruction of your ships.
2207514	In singleplayer missions, you can use Time Compression to speed things up. With Time Compression on, the world moves by at eight times the speed. This is useful in cases such as at the end of a mission while harvesting remaining resources. You can turn Time Compression on or off by pressing the backslash key [\].
2207515	This is the end of the Homeworld: Cataclysm tutorial. All of the topics covered here are also explained in greater detail in the manual, along with helpful quick keys and other useful information. Click the QUIT button to exit.
2207516	Left click an objective to continue.
2207517	Tutorial ends.
2207518	The lesson is now complete. Click the NEXT LESSON button when you are ready to move on.
2207519	Target acquired, Command. Returning.
2207520	The Beast is launching another wave of missiles from that captured transport. It must be converting the refugees into bio-warheads. Destroy it.
2207521	The Hangar Module has been lost. We need to rebuild the Hangar Module as soon as possible.
2207522	We're a big target for those Raider Missile Corvettes. We need some more firepower of our own. Have engineering pull up whatever they can find on missile technology and have them begin researching it now.
2207523	Build a Weapons Module immediately.
2207524	<c=ffffff></c>
2207525	You can still click and hold the RIGHT MOUSE BUTTON to change your view and click and hold BOTH MOUSE BUTTONS - or the MOUSE WHEEL if you have it - to zoom in and out while the Sensors Manager is active.
2207526	While you can move your view however you like, a top down view is often the best overview of your fleet in Sensors Manager.
2207527	Alternately, you can access the Movement Manager by pressing the [M] key. Activate the Movement Manager now...
2207528	The lock down on Movement Manager is now released and you can move your ship.
2207529	This is a good angle to use when in the Movement Manager. It gives you a very clear picture of where everything is in three-dimensional space.
2207530	Tell the ships to move by placing the pointer where you want to go. To move ships vertically, hold the [Shift] key while moving the pointer.
2207531	When the Movement Manager is open, ships in view will have an orange line drawn from them, which indicates their position up or down in relation to the 'pie plate' of the Movement Manager. These lines are also drawn for 'pings' while moving in Sensors Manager.
2207532	If you move the pointer to the base of a height line, then hold down [Shift] to adjust the pointers height, you can use the height line as a guide to help you move the pointer to a specific position relative to another ship.
2207533	KUUN-LAN, this is the CAAL-SHTO. Sensors are reading a slipgate up ahead. It should lead out of this nebula. We are going to make a break for it at best possible speed. Hyperspace when you can and join us on the other side. CAAL-SHTO out.
